userStories.description:
**Goal** — Introduce the API-owned registry, creator-authorized executor and persisted hash-only audit/replay for thread tool calls.

**Motivation** — Approach B (S5b-R5) keeps every permission decision and every user-written byte on the server: the runner holds no user token, and the tool layer is the same one MCP (S5b-3) and the write tools (S5b-2b) build on.

**Approach** — S5b-R5: tools are defined and executed on the koda API; the runner session only proxies calls. No user token and no write capability leave the server. S5b-R6: each tool names the permission its REST route enforces; the executor evaluates it for the acting user's principal on every call. No role-string comparison in tools. S5b-R7: the executor owns the project scope. The project is the thread's project (`FleetJob.projectId`); no tool takes a project argument. Every id in a tool's input must belong to that project; a foreign id answers `not_found` (no existence leak). Replay is the explicit first-step exception to re-evaluation.

`AssistantToolExecutor.execute(call: { jobId; toolCallId; name; input }): Promise<ToolResult>` runs, in order:

1. Replay: an `AssistantToolCall` row for `(jobId, toolCallId)` with the same `tool` and `inputHash` returns its stored `{ content, isError }`; nothing else runs. A row with a different `tool` or `inputHash` answers `invalid_input: toolCallId reused` (not stored).
2. Lookup: an unknown `name` throws `NotFoundAppException` (`fleet.assistantTool`).
3. Context: the job's project (id, slug), thread and creator (`ChatThread.createdById` -> `User`), read by `findCallContext` (1b-D6). A creator whose `User.disabled` is true answers `forbidden: the thread creator is disabled`. The actor is the creator's `UserPrincipal`.
4. Parse: `ToolInputError` -> `invalid_input: <reason>`.
5. Permission: `resolveMembership(projectId, actor)`; a `ForbiddenAppException` -> `forbidden: <tool> needs project membership on <slug>`. For `ability` tools, the ability built by `createForUser(withProjectRole(actor, role))` must allow `(action, subject)`, else `forbidden: <tool> needs <action> <subject> on <slug>`.
6. Execute: `NotFoundAppException` -> `not_found`; any other error -> `error: <tool> failed` (logged with the job id, never returned).
7. Cap: `capText(content, 16384)`.
8. Audit and store: insert `AssistantToolCall`; a unique violation on `(jobId, toolCallId)` (a concurrent duplicate, detected with `isUniqueViolation(error, 'toolCallId')` from `apps/api/src/common/utils/prisma-errors.ts`) returns the stored row's result.

A cap can cut a closing fence line; the result then ends with `[truncated]` and the agent sees an unclosed fence, which the instructions line already marks as data.

Outcomes stored in `AssistantToolCall.outcome`: `ok`, `invalid_input`, `forbidden`, `not_found`, `error`.

`AssistantToolCall` (migration `20261018090000_assistant_tool_call`): `id`, `jobId` (FK FleetJob, cascade), `threadId`, `projectId`, `toolCallId` (string, at most 200 chars), `tool`, `inputHash` (sha256 hex of the input's stable JSON; the input itself is never stored), `outcome`, `content` (text, the capped result), `isError`, `resultBytes`, `durationMs`, `createdAt`; `inputHash` is the sha256 hex of `stableStringify(input)` (`apps/api/src/fleet/sync/stable-json.ts`) over the raw input as the route received it, before `parse`; `@@unique([jobId, toolCallId])`, `@@index([threadId, createdAt])`.

1b-D6 (call context lookup, a recorded exception to the api-data layering rule): no exported service returns a job's thread creator with the user's role, `disabled` flag and the project's slug. The assistant module's own repository, `PrismaAssistantToolCallRepository` (extends `AbstractPrismaRepository` over `AssistantToolCall`, behind the `ASSISTANT_TOOL_CALL_REPOSITORY` token, never exported), has one escape-hatch read, `findCallContext(jobId)`, that reads `FleetJob` -> `ChatThread` -> creator `User` and the job's `Project` in one query, as `prisma-chat-thread.repository.ts` already reads `FleetRepo` and `Project` rows. Everything else goes through services.

1b-D7 (registry wiring): `AssistantToolRegistry` is an injectable with `register(...tools: AssistantTool[]): void` (throws `Error` on a duplicate name) and `get(name: string): AssistantTool | undefined`. Each tool group is an injectable class in `AssistantModule` (`TicketTools` in US-003, `FleetTools` in US-004) that injects the services it needs and the registry, and calls `registry.register(...)` in `onModuleInit`. Each group appends its descriptors to `KODA_TOOL_DESCRIPTORS`; a tool's `descriptor` is the entry of its name there.

1b-D2 (tool outcomes are data): a call that reaches a tool answers HTTP 200 with `{ content, isError }`. Tool-level refusals (`invalid_input`, `forbidden`, `not_found`, `error`) are `isError: true` results the agent reads. HTTP errors are reserved for the route's own refusals (lease, kill switch, unknown tool, malformed query).

1b-D3 (descriptors are static data): the tool descriptors (`name`, `description`, `inputSchema`) live in a plain module, `apps/api/src/fleet/assistant/koda-tool-descriptors.ts`, exported as `KODA_TOOL_DESCRIPTORS`. The placement code imports the constant directly, so `FleetJobsModule` never imports the assistant module (which imports `FleetJobsModule` for `FleetJobsService`): no module cycle.

US-002 creates `koda-tool-descriptors.ts` with an empty `KODA_TOOL_DESCRIPTORS`; US-003 and US-004 append their descriptors to it and register their tools with the registry. US-002 also creates `assistant.module.spec.ts`, whose compile test imports `GlobalStubsModule` with `AssistantModule`; US-003, US-004 and US-005 grow the module's imports and providers and may add stub providers that test needs, keeping its one assertion (it resolves `AssistantToolExecutor`). Add AssistantModule to FleetModule, preserve fleet.module.spec.ts assertions and existing global stubs, and add fleet.assistantTool translations (English: Unknown koda tool) in both API locales. Use one 16-hex-character randomBytes(8) token per call and implement ctx.data as maskSecrets then fenceData with that token.

**Scope** — In: types/ToolInputError, registry, executor, private audit repository and context lookup, empty static descriptors, schema/migration, module wiring and tests. Out: concrete tool groups and their service exports (US-003/004), HTTP route/placement/notice (US-005), runner proxies (US-006), web notice (US-007); no stored raw inputs, audit retention UI, per-runner throttling or session termination on disabled-creator refusal. The concurrent unique-violation behavior is implemented, but a two-concurrent-first-call test is out of scope.

**Interface** —
```ts
// assistant-tool.types.ts
export type ToolPermission = { kind: 'member' } | { kind: 'ability'; action: string; subject: string };
export interface ToolContext {
  projectId: string; projectSlug: string; actor: UserPrincipal; threadId: string; jobId: string; now: Date;
  /** Masks secrets, then wraps the text in this call's fence. Every user-written field goes through it. */
  data(text: string): string;
}
export interface AssistantTool<I = unknown> {
  descriptor: KodaToolDescriptor;   // one of KODA_TOOL_DESCRIPTORS
  permission: ToolPermission;
  parse(input: unknown): I;         // throws ToolInputError(reason)
  execute(ctx: ToolContext, input: I): Promise<string>;
}
export interface ToolResult { content: string; isError: boolean }
```
`AssistantToolExecutor.execute(call: { jobId; toolCallId; name; input }): Promise<ToolResult>`; `register(...tools: AssistantTool[]): void`; `get(name: string): AssistantTool | undefined`.

**Test implementation** — Strict three-session TDD; fake tools drive unit authorization, masking, cap and error mapping, real PostgreSQL drives audit/raw-hash and sequential replay through a seeded THREAD job. Keep module compilation a colocated unit test with GlobalStubsModule and no DB. Run integrations via test:scoped with the compose test database available; do not count skipped DB suites as validation.
